cumbency": 2.0, "money": 2.05}, "rating_margin": 12.89, "sigma_race": 6.8, "sigma_total": 7.39, "baseline_margin": 12.11, "analyst_adjustment": 0.39, "key_factors": ["GOP nominee Thomas Smith won the primary despite having suspended his campaign, then drew scandal coverage over radical social media posts", "Kristen McDonald Rivet holds a massive financial edge: $5.6M raised and $4.3M on hand versus roughly $244K for Smith", "Expert ratings cluster between Likely and Safe D, and prediction markets price the incumbent around 92-93%", "No public general-election polling in a district that only leans slightly Republican keeps uncertainty elevated"], "rationale": "Every available signal points the same way: raters call the seat Likely-to-Safe Democratic, markets price the incumbent in the low 90s, and the Republican nominee enters the fall with almost no money and a self-inflicted scandal after winning a primary he had already quit. The main restraint is the absence of any polling in a district that leans only marginally Republican, which argues against calling the race out of reach.", "watch": "Any general-election polling showing Smith consolidating Republican voters, or significant outside spending arriving on his behalf, would be the clearest sign the race is tightening.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Incumbent Kristen McDonald Rivet (D) is running."]}, "overview": "First-term Democrat Kristen McDonald Rivet seeks re-election in a mid-Michigan district that leans only slightly Republican. She enters the general election with a dominant financial position, having raised $5.6 million and holding $4.3 million on hand, while Republican nominee Thomas Smith has raised roughly $244,000.\n\nSmith became the nominee in unusual circumstances, winning the primary even though he had suspended his campaign, and he has since faced critical coverage over radical social media posts that led him to delete his accounts, plus criticism for skipping a debate forum. No public general-election polls are available, but nine expert ratings place the race between Likely and Safe Democratic, and prediction markets price the incumbent's chances in the low 90s. The evidence points to a clear Democratic favorite against an underfunded, scandal-drawn opponent.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.9646, "margin": 12.11}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.9617, "margin": 12.5}]}